  # - family-names: [Co-author]
  #   given-names: [Name]
abstract: >-
  The Reef Restoration Prioritisation Tool is a free, open-source R Shiny
  application that turns routine benthic transect surveys into relative,
  site-level restoration priorities for reef managers working with limited
  data and resources. For each site it calculates a Feasibility index
  (relative ecological condition) and an Urgency index (relative pressure),
  places the sites on a decision plane and assigns each to one of four
  management classes with a suggested intervention pathway: A, Assess for
  active restoration; B, Address barriers to recovery; C, Protect and
  maintain; D, Monitor and reassess. Absolute alerts flag coral cover below
  10%, severe bleaching and extensive rubble. The tool reports bootstrap
  uncertainty for every score and class, tests how far the classes depend
  on the indicator weights, produces a donor-site shortlist, and can add
  trends from repeated surveys. It accepts a single CSV file or Reef Check
  substrate data, runs in a web browser or locally in R, and saves a
  settings file so that each analysis can be repeated. It is intended as a
  first-pass screening step to support expert and stakeholder decisions,
  not as a measure of restoration success or a forecast of coral loss. It
  was developed with monitoring data from Isla del Caño Biological Reserve,
  Costa Rica.
keywords:
  - coral reef
  - restoration
  - decision support
  - R Shiny
  - Eastern Tropical Pacific
repository-code: "https://github.com/CarolineVPalmer/Reef_Restoration_Tool"
